**user**:
> In pivot_wider, can I change the naming of columns? I have a call:
> ```
>     info %>% group_by(scenario, time) %>% summarize(mu=mean(var), med=median(var), ci25=quantile(var, .25), ci75=quantile(var, .75)) %>%
>         pivot_wider(names_from='scenario', values_from=c('mu', 'med', 'ci25', 'ci75'), names_sort=F)
> ```
> And I get columns like `mu_Baseline`, but I want columns like `Baseline_mu`.
**assistant**:
> Yes, you can change the naming of columns in `pivot_wider` using the `names_glue` argument. The `names_glue` argument allows you to specify a custom naming pattern for your new columns. In your case, you want to swap the order of the `scenario` and the statistical measure name (`mu`, `med`, `ci25`, `ci75`).
> 
> Here's how you can modify your `pivot_wider` call to achieve the desired column names `Baseline_mu`, `Baseline_med`, etc.:
> 
> ```r
> info %>% 
>   group_by(scenario, time) %>% 
>   summarize(
>     mu = mean(var), 
>     med = median(var), 
>     ci25 = quantile(var, .25), 
>     ci75 = quantile(var, .75)
>   ) %>% 
>   pivot_wider(
>     names_from = 'scenario', 
>     values_from = c('mu', 'med', 'ci25', 'ci75'), 
>     names_sort = FALSE,
>     names_glue = "{scenario}_{.value}"
>   )
> ```
> 
> In the `names_glue` argument, `{scenario}` will be replaced by the value from `names_from`, and `{.value}` will be replaced by the values from `values_from`. This will rearrange the names to the format you desire.
**user**:
> Can I also make these so that they're grouped? So, I get the final dataframe:
> ```
> # A tibble: 3 × 13
>    time Baseline_mu IntegratedAction_mu LTCAction_mu Baseline_med IntegratedAction_med LTCAction_med Baseline_ci25 IntegratedAction_ci25 LTCAction_ci25
>   <dbl>       <dbl>               <dbl>        <dbl>        <dbl>                <dbl>         <dbl>         <dbl>                 <dbl>          <dbl>
> ```
> But I want to see mu_IntegratedAction, med_IntegratedAction, ci25_IntegratedAction, before moving on to mu_Baseline, etc.
**assistant**:
> Yes, you can achieve that by using the `names_glue` argument along with sorting your `names_from` to change the order of how `scenario` names appear in the final column names. You would need to arrange your `scenario` values in the desired order before calling `pivot_wider`.
> 
> Here's how you can modify your code to get the final output with the desired grouping of columns:
> 
> ```r
> library(dplyr)
> library(tidyr)
> 
> info %>%
>   group_by(scenario, time) %>%
>   summarize(
>     mu = mean(var), 
>     med = median(var), 
>     ci25 = quantile(var, .25), 
>     ci75 = quantile(var, .75)
>   ) %>%
>   ungroup() %>%
>   pivot_wider(
>     names_from = 'scenario', 
>     values_from = c('mu', 'med', 'ci25', 'ci75'), 
>     names_glue = "{.value}_{scenario}",
>     names_sort = TRUE
>   ) %>%
>   # Reorder columns as needed
>   select(time, 
>          contains("IntegratedAction"), 
>          contains("Baseline"), 
>          contains("LTCAction"))
> ```
> 
> ### Breakdown of the Code:
> 
> 1. **`names_glue`:** This pattern `{.value}_{scenario}` will create names like `mu_IntegratedAction`, `med_IntegratedAction`, etc.
>    
> 2. **`names_sort`:** Setting this to `TRUE` ensures that the columns are sorted alphabetically after naming.
> 
> 3. **`select()`:** After the pivoting, you can reorder the columns as desired. The `contains()` function is helpful for grouping similar columns together.
> 
> With this setup, your final dataframe should reflect the structured grouping you want!
